<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk" TreatAsLocalProperty="Version">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <!--
      The application's own version, and not the toolkit's, which the rest of this repository takes. An exported
      access file names the project a row access contribution is in with its version, so that a new version of
      what writes the policies is a new file. With the toolkit's version here, every release of the toolkit
      would write the sample's three access files anew, with nothing in them changed but that number.

      TreatAsLocalProperty, on the Project element, is what keeps it this project's own in a release build. That
      build gives the toolkit's version on the command line (-p:Version), and a property given there wins over
      the one a project file sets, unless the project says the property is local. Without it the release build,
      which only compares the exported files, would find the access files missing and fail.
    -->
    <Version>1.0.0</Version>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The application's permission catalogue, and the starter project roles of its tenants' crews, in a project of
    its own because two programs need them: the host, which runs with them, and the program that exports the
    database's policies, which are written from them. It is no module and declares none: its packs name the
    modules' keys, which their contracts and application projects publish, and it knows nothing of how any module
    is stored. The keys themselves it does not list: Tenancy's generator collects them, below.

    Next to them is what this application's two supporting domains write into the exported access files:
    SampleTenancyContribution, Tenancy's, and ProjectMembershipFunctions, Membership's for the projects, both
    offered here and used by the project that exports.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The keys the packs name, and each module's own part of the catalogue. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Modules\Projects\Examples.Tenancy.Projects.Application\Examples.Tenancy.Projects.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Modules\Inspections\Examples.Tenancy.Inspections.Application\Examples.Tenancy.Inspections.Application.csproj" />
    <!-- The token role of the application's operators. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Modules\Tenants\Examples.Tenancy.Tenants.Contracts\Examples.Tenancy.Tenants.Contracts.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\Source\DDDToolkit.Supporting.Tenancy\DDDToolkit.Supporting.Tenancy.csproj" />
    <!-- TenancyRowAccessContribution, which the contribution here closes over the catalogue. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\Source\DDDToolkit.Supporting.Tenancy.Postgres\DDDToolkit.Supporting.Tenancy.Postgres.csproj" />
    <!-- MembershipRowAccessContribution, which the contribution here closes over the projects' rules. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\Source\DDDToolkit.Supporting.Membership.Postgres\DDDToolkit.Supporting.Membership.Postgres.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Tenancy's generator, which a NuGet consumer gets with the Tenancy package and this repository wires by hand. It
    writes TenancyPermissionsOfModules into this project, which declares no module: every key the modules referenced
    above mark with [TenancyPermissions], what the catalogue the export writes from is built with.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\Source\DDDToolkit.Supporting.Tenancy.Analyzers\DDDToolkit.Supporting.Tenancy.Analyzers.csproj" OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
